##DESCRIPTION
##  A problem to the expectation of the square of a normal random variable.
##ENDDESCRIPTION

## DBsubject(Statistics)
## DBchapter(Confidence intervals)
## DBsection(One sample mean - z)
## Date(10/19/2012)
## Institution(The University of British Columbia)
## Author(Djun Kim)
## Level(3)
## MO(1)
## KEYWORDS('statistics', 'continuous random variables', 'expectation')

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

# Setup

Context("Interval");

@data = (183, 222, 303, 262, 178, 232, 268, 201, 244, 183, 201, 140);

$sum = 0;
$sum += $_ for (@data);
$mean = Real($sum / @data);

$conf_int =
    Interval($mean - (1.96 * 50 / sqrt(12)), $mean + (1.96 * 50 / sqrt(12)));

@b_hours = (25, 26, 27, 29, 30, 31);
$b_ind   = random(0, 5);
$b_hours = @b_hours[$b_ind];
$part_b  = Real(ceil(((2 * 1.96 * 50) / $b_hours)**2));

$tab = DataTable([ [@data] ], align => '|' . ('c|') x 12, horizontalrules => 1);

BEGIN_PGML
The time in hours for a worker to repair an electrical instrument is a Normally distributed random variable with a mean of [`\mu`] and a standard deviation of 50. The repair times for 12 such instruments chosen at random are as follows:

[$tab]*

a) Find a 95% confidence interval for [` \mu `]. Use at least one digit of accuracy.

    [_]{$conf_int->cmp(tolType=>'tolType', tolerance => 0.1)}

b) Find the least number of repair times needed to be sampled in order to reduce the width of the confidence interval to below [$b_hours] hours.  

    [_]{$part_b->cmp(tol=>'0.001' , tolType=>'absolute')}

[@ helpLink('interval') @]*

END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION

a) Let [` \bar{x}`] be the sample mean of the data.  Then the confidence interval can be written:

    [``` \begin{aligned}
    \text{Conf. Int} & = \bar{x} \pm 1.96 \times \frac{\sigma}{\sqrt{n}}  \\
    & = [$mean] \pm 1.96 \times \frac{50}{\sqrt{12}}
    \end{aligned}
    ```]
    which is [`[$conf_int]`]. 

b) We wish for the width [` = 2 \times 1.96 \times \frac{50}{\sqrt{n}} < [$b_hours] `] so we find

    [``` 
    \begin{aligned}
    2 \times 1.96 \times 50 & < [$b_hours] \sqrt{n} \\
    \sqrt{n} & > [@ 2*1.96*50/$b_hours @] \\
    n & > [@ (2*1.96*50/$b_hours)**2 @]
    \end{aligned}
    ```]
    Since this needs to be an integer, we choose [`n = [$part_b] `]

END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
